# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every TypeScript change. `scripts/check` already enforces
formatting, the compiler's strict flags, the lint rules, unused code and the test rules, so they
are not repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain TypeScript

Use type aliases, discriminated unions, plain objects and functions. A class only when state and
behaviour belong together. Generics beyond one type parameter, conditional and mapped types,
decorators and new runtime libraries need the owner's approval first. Once approved, they go into
the list.

Approved extras:

- None.

## Comments

A reader can follow a module from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every module starts with a comment that says what the module is for.
- Every exported definition has a `/** ... */` comment that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a TypeScript construct briefly the first time a module uses it, if the reader may not
  know it. For example a type guard, `satisfies` or a discriminated union. One short sentence,
  sometimes with the equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Values and errors

- Named exports only. The default export of `vitest.config.ts` is the one exception.
- Parameters that take arrays take `readonly` arrays.
- Expected failures are values: return `{ ok: true, value } | { ok: false, error }`. Throw only an
  `Error`, and only for the unexpected, such as a full disk.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting.
- Names say what a value is: `environment`, `argument`. Single letters only in tiny lambdas.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new entry in `dependencies` or `devDependencies` in `package.json`, or in `allowBuilds` in
`pnpm-workspace.yaml`, needs the owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- Test names describe behaviour.
- Review what the checks cannot see: a type guard (`value is T`) or an overload signature that
  claims more than the code checks, a `catch` that turns every error into a default value, a
  deleted or weakened test, a test that returns before its real assertion, catches its own
  assertion error or mocks the module it tests, an export or method that only tests use, and a
  devDependency imported from `src/`.
- A rule that holds for many inputs gets a property test with fast-check, as in
  `tests/{{package}}.test.ts`.
